WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:08.130
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: We have started today a quarter
of an hour later than we normally do because

00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:13.920
of the observance of a silence for the victims
of the Manchester bombing. We will have a

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:18.840
few more words to say about that in the course
of the meeting, and of the NHS response to

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:19.840
it.

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:24.860
But may I start, please, by formally opening
the meeting. I have had apologies from only

00:00:24.860 --> 00:00:31.470
one director, and that is Joanne Shaw. I need
to invite any members of the Board to review,

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:35.940
please, their declarations of interest, all
of which are tabled in front of us, and to

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:42.140
advise now if there are any specific interests
relevant to any item on the agenda.

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:46.380
No, we have none. Thank you very much.

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:52.820
I should, again, observe that there is no
formal expectation of a fire alarm test this

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:57.990
morning, so that if the alarm should go off,
please follow the advice and instructions

00:00:57.990 --> 00:01:02.270
of our staff, who will escort us safely from the premises.

00:01:02.270 --> 00:01:07.900
We have, now, the minutes of the last meeting
of the Board. Could I please ask colleagues

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:13.250
to confirm whether they are a true and accurate
record? They are, thank you. I've had no indication

00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:17.260
of any other business; is there any other
business? No, thank you very much.

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:23.800
Sorry, I should have said 'any matters arising';
I'm jumping ahead of myself.

00:01:23.810 --> 00:01:30.570
So, let me turn then to the Chairman's Report.
I'd like to start off by observing that we're

00:01:30.570 --> 00:01:36.820
holding the Board under conditions of purdah,
and that that is a cross-governmental requirement

00:01:36.820 --> 00:01:43.299
which inevitably cuts in at the time that
a general election is called. Had the election

00:01:43.299 --> 00:01:46.600
timing been somewhat different, I think we
would have rescheduled the meeting of the

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:49.950
Board, but as it is, I think we have to do
two things.

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:54.790
One is to be respectful of purdah; the other
is to try to ensure that it doesn't conflict

00:01:54.790 --> 00:02:00.860
with our requirements of good governance,
as a board conducting serious public business

00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:02.380
and conducting it in public.

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:08.920
The way in which we have dealt with that in
assembling the agenda today requires us to

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:15.099
make some amendments to how we would normally
have proceeded. I would suggest that probably

00:02:15.099 --> 00:02:20.480
those which are the most significant are that,
in relation to the items on the agenda under

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:26.950
the heading 'Governance', we have four items:
on the complaints policy, on managing conflicts

00:02:26.950 --> 00:02:31.980
of interest, on standards of business and
on external whistleblowing, in which I would

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:37.790
like to propose to the Board that we should
have a full and open discussion of these issues,

00:02:37.790 --> 00:02:42.650
but that the precise policies, we have felt,
on advice, that we should not put up onto

00:02:42.650 --> 00:02:51.570
the public web at the moment, because of the
potential of breaching the purdah requirements.

00:02:51.570 --> 00:02:56.349
I think it behoves us to be very cautious
about purdah, given the high salience of the

00:02:56.349 --> 00:03:04.150
NHS in any general election, so I should apologise
to members of the public that the policies

00:03:04.150 --> 00:03:08.650
will not themselves be available, but that
will not, I hope, inhibit a full discussion

00:03:08.650 --> 00:03:13.180
around the Board table.

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:18.379
The other two or three items that I think
one should record right at the beginning of

00:03:18.379 --> 00:03:23.390
our discussions this morning are, first of
all, the publication of the Next Steps on

00:03:23.390 --> 00:03:28.780
the Five Year Forward View. That was being
finalised at the time of our last meeting,

00:03:28.780 --> 00:03:33.400
and it is now a substantive agenda item on
today's agenda.

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:43.569
Thirdly, the cybersecurity attack, which affected
the NHS very seriously ten days ago; I will

00:03:43.569 --> 00:03:49.880
invite Simon to say a little more about that
in his Chief Executive's Remarks. But to remark

00:03:49.880 --> 00:03:56.049
only at this stage that it, I think, demonstrated
two clear things: one, the resilience of the

00:03:56.049 --> 00:04:02.150
NHS to the attack; and two, the vulnerability
of the NHS to future potential attacks, and

00:04:02.150 --> 00:04:08.380
to assure the Board that every possible step
is being taken to strengthen that resilience,

00:04:08.380 --> 00:04:13.280
working not only through NHS England but through
the arm's-length bodies and the Department

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:15.270
of Health.

00:04:15.270 --> 00:04:22.620
And then fourthly to, again, make mention
of the tragic bombing in Manchester, and the

00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:28.830
unfortunate and devastating loss of life and
really devastating injuries for those in NHS

00:04:28.830 --> 00:04:35.910
care still. Again, Simon will comment further
on that, but as an exhibition of the solidarity

00:04:35.910 --> 00:04:43.400
which the NHS represents in contemporary society,
it's a very, I think, striking reminder of

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:46.440
the ultimate function that we're all here
to fulfil.

00:04:47.980 --> 00:04:54.120
Let me then turn briefly to update the Board
on some of the activities with which I as

00:04:54.130 --> 00:04:57.360
Chair have been involved since the last meeting.

00:04:57.360 --> 00:05:03.530
A series of public speaking engagements, including
work that I've been doing with the Academy

00:05:03.530 --> 00:05:09.130
of Social Sciences leading up to the publication
of their report on Health to the People, trying

00:05:09.130 --> 00:05:13.759
to understand better how the social sciences
as well as the life sciences have a role to

00:05:13.759 --> 00:05:20.850
play in promoting public health and reduction
of mortality and morbidity. A presentation

00:05:20.850 --> 00:05:27.850
to the Dementia Conference organised by the
Alzheimer's Society last week; again, emphasising

00:05:27.850 --> 00:05:34.210
the relationship of the work that NHSE does
in the much broader context of social care,

00:05:34.210 --> 00:05:42.509
and other interventions that are critical
to coping with a major episode of - and growing

00:05:42.509 --> 00:05:47.330
episode of cognitive decline across society.

00:05:47.330 --> 00:05:53.720
Secondly, a number of visits, including to
Moorfields, and also discussions with the

00:05:53.720 --> 00:06:01.561
Royal Free Hospital on the use of modern technologies
to advance the interests of patients. And

00:06:01.561 --> 00:06:08.100
also, a recent visit to the Altogether Better
vanguard at Dudley, accompanied by Sir Bruce

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:13.949
Keogh and Sam Jones, to try to understand
where our best-performing multi-specialty

00:06:13.949 --> 00:06:20.720
community practices providers were heading
as we move towards an era in which STPs will

00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:25.580
absorb a number of those functions and those
concepts, and take them further.

00:06:25.580 --> 00:06:32.550
And then thirdly, a number of cross-governmental
meetings working on the Genomics England Board

00:06:32.550 --> 00:06:37.740
and on the 100,000 Genomes programme. Meetings
with the Department of Health across government,

00:06:37.740 --> 00:06:44.380
with the new National Guardian on speaking
up, with the Policy and Health Services Commissioner,

00:06:44.380 --> 00:06:51.039
and working also with the team on the Life
Sciences Strategy. So, that is intended simply

00:06:51.040 --> 00:06:55.440
to keep the Board abreast with activity conducted
as Chair.

00:06:56.180 --> 00:07:03.180
I have no further matters to report. I would
invite the Board to express any views, comments

00:07:03.180 --> 00:07:09.090
or queries on that report, but in the absence
of that, invite you please to note it, and

00:07:09.090 --> 00:07:13.640
to turn now to the Chief Executive's Report.
Simon, over to you.

00:07:13.640 --> 00:07:18.539
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Thank you, Malcolm. So, in
a similar vein, given purdah, this will - we'll

00:07:18.539 --> 00:07:24.669
confine ourselves to immediate operational
matters. And, as you say, I want to really

00:07:24.669 --> 00:07:29.440
focus on the work that's happened since our
last Board meeting, in responding both to

00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:35.449
the cyber attack and the Manchester outrage.
And as you cast your mind back, you'll remember

00:07:35.449 --> 00:07:39.721
that eight weeks ago, at our public Board
meeting, we actually reviewed the NHS Emergency

00:07:39.721 --> 00:07:45.880
Preparedness and Response arrangements, and
the training and the drills that are regularly

00:07:45.880 --> 00:07:52.289
run to ensure that they operate smoothly.
Unfortunately, since that meeting at the end

00:07:52.289 --> 00:08:01.479
of March, we've had these two major incidents:
firstly, the cyber attack, which we stood

00:08:01.479 --> 00:08:08.000
up a national response to, beginning at 16.00
on Friday, 12th May, which lasted a week.

00:08:08.000 --> 00:08:11.250
And then secondly, Manchester.

00:08:11.250 --> 00:08:16.380
In respect of cyber, I think the first thing
to say is that this was probably the first

00:08:16.380 --> 00:08:26.960
national-scale threat that the NHS has received,
and some very effective work took place in

00:08:26.960 --> 00:08:30.990
response to that. I think there are a number
of lessons that NHS Digital, NHS England,

00:08:30.990 --> 00:08:35.729
NHS Improvement, the National Cybersecurity
Centre, will be taking from that. Some of

00:08:35.729 --> 00:08:42.289
them we have discussed as a board, in private,
for obvious reasons, due to the security-related

00:08:42.289 --> 00:08:50.730
aspects of that, but there's work that, under
the leadership of NHS Digital and colleagues,

00:08:50.730 --> 00:08:55.910
we will be needing to take as a system over
the course of the next sort of several weeks

00:08:55.910 --> 00:08:58.080
and months.

00:08:58.080 --> 00:09:04.290
But I really just want to actually talk more
about the Manchester response. And as everybody

00:09:04.290 --> 00:09:09.940
knows, the regional emergency coordination
process immediately kicked in on Monday night,

00:09:09.940 --> 00:09:11.640
has worked practically flawlessly.

00:09:11.640 --> 00:09:20.830
There have been effective trial runs and processes
put in place across the major trauma hospital

00:09:20.830 --> 00:09:28.420
and ambulance service networks that have been
rehearsed very recently, in fact. As of 06.00

00:09:28.420 --> 00:09:34.940
this morning, there are 116 people who've
received inpatient care in relation to the

00:09:34.940 --> 00:09:40.440
incident, and 23 people are still in critical
care.

00:09:41.830 --> 00:09:48.500
The NHS is also mobilising appropriate support
for children and young people, families and

00:09:48.510 --> 00:09:56.120
staff, in terms of the psychological consequences
of this. And the Board knows that JTAC raised

00:09:56.120 --> 00:10:01.420
the threat level from severe to critical earlier
in the week, so NHS England has stood up the

00:10:01.420 --> 00:10:09.410
national EPRR requirement that every NHS organisation
now takes action in the way that we have specified

00:10:09.410 --> 00:10:15.790
according to longstanding response protocols,
ensuring that all their staff are aware of

00:10:15.790 --> 00:10:21.810
what the local incident response arrangements
need to be; that they've reviewed, in the

00:10:21.810 --> 00:10:28.930
case of acute hospitals, their operating theatre,
emergency staffing levels, blood supplies,

00:10:28.930 --> 00:10:33.950
ambulance and transport arrangements; and
also confirmed what's called the mutual aid

00:10:33.950 --> 00:10:39.470
arrangements between different NHS organisations
in a geography.

00:10:39.470 --> 00:10:43.339
And I think, perhaps finally, Malcolm, what
I would say is that I think that term 'mutual

00:10:43.339 --> 00:10:49.740
aid' kind of captures the essence of what
we've seen in Manchester, not just between

00:10:49.740 --> 00:10:56.860
different organisations coming together to
respond, but mutual aid as really the embodiment

00:10:56.860 --> 00:11:02.010
of what the NHS has been doing for the people
of this country. And I don't think it can

00:11:02.010 --> 00:11:07.050
be put better than the way in which a senior
doctor at Manchester Royal Infirmary involved

00:11:07.050 --> 00:11:14.010
in treating patients put it when he has said,
'An atrocity has been carried out which has

00:11:14.010 --> 00:11:19.810
had an unmeasurable impact on countless lives.
Many casualties were brought to our department,

00:11:19.810 --> 00:11:25.180
and dozens upon dozens of my fellow staff
arrived to help. The response represented

00:11:25.180 --> 00:11:31.330
everything great about this country: compassion,
empathy, tireless self-sacrifice and, above

00:11:31.330 --> 00:11:37.680
all, profound unity.' So, I think we as NHS
England should put on record our huge debt

00:11:37.680 --> 00:11:42.920
of gratitude once again to the frontline staff
of the National Health Service who have responded

00:11:42.920 --> 00:11:45.290
so brilliantly under these terrible circumstances.

00:11:47.140 --> 00:11:51.380
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Indeed, thank you, Simon.
We will certainly record that. May I invite

00:11:51.380 --> 00:11:57.930
any comments or questions to the Chief Executive?
No. Thank you. We'll note that report.

00:12:00.580 --> 00:12:06.420
May we move on please, then, to the next item,
which is a report on the Next Steps on the

00:12:06.420 --> 00:12:08.260
NHS Five Year Forward View. Simon?

00:12:09.660 --> 00:12:13.860
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: So, for the reasons you said,
Malcolm, this is really a sort of formal report

00:12:13.860 --> 00:12:22.380
in that, at our March 30th meeting, we were
one day before publication of this, and the

00:12:22.380 --> 00:12:28.600
content constitutes our business plan, our
operating plan, for 2017/2018 in line with

00:12:28.600 --> 00:12:37.670
the mandate we are operating under. I think
there is now great clarity about the things

00:12:37.670 --> 00:12:43.050
that we can see need to get done on urgent
and emergency care, and primary care, cancer,

00:12:43.050 --> 00:12:50.400
mental health, the 10-Point Efficiency Plan
that the NHS has laid out. And without pre-empting

00:12:50.400 --> 00:12:58.620
whatever may happen on June 8th, I don't think
there's anything in here which would not be

00:12:58.620 --> 00:13:04.579
important for the NHS to be responding to
over the course of the coming 12 and 24 months.

00:13:04.579 --> 00:13:11.050
So, notwithstanding the election campaign
going on, in practice the whole of the NHS

00:13:11.050 --> 00:13:14.779
is mobilising around making sure we're as
well placed as we can be to get this stuff

00:13:14.779 --> 00:13:20.370
done for the patients and communities of this
country over the course of the next year.

00:13:20.370 --> 00:13:26.580
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thank you. I'm looking around
to see whether I should invite any comments,

00:13:26.580 --> 00:13:31.149
but I am conscious that every member of this
Board has played a major part in devising

00:13:31.149 --> 00:13:37.350
the Next Steps document, and the non-executives
have been deeply involved in this throughout

00:13:37.350 --> 00:13:42.860
the process. So, unless anybody has anything
further to add at this stage, may I actually

00:13:42.860 --> 00:13:47.210
commend the Executive on behalf of the Board
for the production of the report; to stress

00:13:47.210 --> 00:13:52.690
secondly its interdependence with other arm's-length
bodies and other players across the system

00:13:52.690 --> 00:13:58.110
across for delivery; and thirdly, to note
that we will be monitoring the progress of

00:13:58.110 --> 00:14:03.720
its implementation and delivery across the
country very closely. Are we content to note

00:14:03.720 --> 00:14:04.780
that report? Thank you.

